# Tasks Rules

## Shortcode Syntax

Use it as interlinking between `#tasks`, `#procedures`, `#runbooks`, `#lessons`, `#decisions` (ADR) instead of providing paths.

Format: `[relation:type:identifier]` or `[type:identifier]` when relation is implied

Examples:
- `[task:001]` – reference to task
- `[adr:003]` – reference to decision
- `[lesson:context-overload]` – reference to lesson
- `[procedure:task-achieving]` – reference to procedure
- `used_in:[task:005, content:tooltips]` – used in multiple places
- `depends_on:[adr:001, task:003]` – dependencies

### `#resolver` CLI (`#shortcode` resolution)

Use the `#resolver` to map `#shortcode`s to file paths (requires up-to-date `#index`es):

- Generate indexes first (if needed): `npm run index:all`
- Resolve by positional args:
  - `npm run resolve -- tasks 010` → `.cursor/tasks/task-010.mdc`
- Resolve by flags:
  - `npm run resolve -- --kind tasks --id 010`
  - `npm run resolve -- --kind rules --id mcp-future`

Notes:
- For `#tasks`, `010` maps to `task-010.mdc`. You can also use `task:010`.
- For other kinds, `#resolver` matches by filename or `name` in the header.

## Task Metadata (MDC Header)

```yaml
---
name: string              # Required – descriptive task name
number: integer           # Required – unique ID (can be auto-generated)
status: enum              # Required – [idea|backlog|task|wip|done|deferred]
type: enum                # [feature|bugfix|refactor|chore|docs|test|style|perf]
scope: enum               # [docs|design|deploy|build|content|code]
priority: enum            # [low|medium|high|critical]
effort: enum              # [xs|sm|md|lg|xl]
created: date             # YYYY-MM-DD (ISO)
modified: date            # YYYY-MM-DD (ISO)
dependencies: array       # [type:id, type:id]
related_tasks: array      # [task:NNN, task:MMM]
context_files: array      # [path/to/file.mdc]
tags: array               # [tag1, tag2, tag3]
resolution: string        # For done/deferred: [accepted|rejected|deferred|completed]
---
```

## Task Metadata

### Required Fields
- `name` – Descriptive task name
- `number` – Unique 3-digit integer (001, 002, ...)
- `status` – Current state [idea|backlog|ready-to-do|task|wip|done|deferred]

### Recommended Fields
- `type` – [feature|bugfix|refactor|chore|docs|test|style|perf]
- `created` – ISO date when task was created

### Optional Fields
- `scope`, `priority`, `effort`, `modified`, `dependencies`, `related_tasks`, `context_files`, `tags`, `resolution`

Add fields as needed for your workflow.

## From `#creator`

every `#task` must go via stages:
- `#idea` (from `#developer` or `#agent`)
- `#backlog` – created and filled; architects and scrum masters prepare/refine, then move to `#ready-to-do`, then `#wip` when working, then next. The `#refinement` process is critical.
- Always follow the `#task` planning and `#refinement` process; do not bypass `#procedures` or `#status` transitions.
- Act automatically, following `#procedures` and transitioning `#status` of `#tasks` correctly.
- Structure `#creator` inputs into your instructions.

## `#DoR` – Definition of Ready

A `#task` is considered defined and `#ready-to-do` ONLY when all of the following are true:

- `#problem` statement: clear, unambiguous, written for mid-level devs unfamiliar with the repo.
- `#business-value`: one or two sentences of why this matters.
- `#acceptance-criteria`: concrete, testable outcomes (bullet list).
- `#scope` boundaries: what is in/out; explicit constraints and dependencies.
- `#references`: `#shortcode`s to related rules/procedures/lessons/ADRs.
- `#commit-plan`: proposed high-level commits (Conventional Commits style) or steps.

`#placeholders` such as "Describe the task, related items, and context here." are FORBIDDEN. `#tasks` containing `#placeholders` MUST NOT be moved to `#ready-to-do` or executed.

## Procedures and Artifacts

- Define tasks under `.cursor/tasks/` using the metadata above.
- Follow `[procedure:task-defining]` to create tasks (see `./.cursor/procedures/task-defining.mdc`).
- Follow `[procedure:task-achieving]` to complete tasks (see `./.cursor/procedures/task-achieving.mdc`).
- Runbook is required only for significant tasks; trivial chores may omit runbook but should still update status and key notes.
- If a reusable insight emerges, extract a `#lesson` into `.cursor/lessons/` and cross-link.

### Execution Policy

- Do not start work outside a `task-NNN.mdc`.
- Refine the `#task` until it is `#ready-to-do` (according to `[procedure:task-defining]`) before execution.
- Use the specific `#procedures`: `task-defining.mdc` for setup, `task-achieving.mdc` for execution.
- Maintain a `#runbook` during/after execution for significant `#tasks` and keep `#status` up to date.
- After completion, update the `#task` body (final `#scope`, list of changes) and add `modified` (ISO date).

### `#validation-gate`

- Moving a `#task` into `#ready-to-do` or starting execution requires passing validation:
  - `pnpm run index:all` (or `npm run index:all`) and `npm run validate:indexes` must succeed locally.
  - Validation fails if the task contains placeholder text or lacks required sections (Problem, Business value, Acceptance criteria, Scope).

### Interlinking Requirement

- Always interlink artifacts using `#shortcode`s: `#tasks` ↔ `#procedures` ↔ `#runbooks` ↔ `#lessons`.
- Example: In task body, reference `[procedure:task-achieving]`; in runbook, reference `[task:NNN]` and any `[lesson:name]`.

## Indexing and Automation

- INDEX files (e.g., `INDEX.json`) are generated by scripts, not hand-maintained, and used by the agent for overview.
- See `./.cursor/rules/mcp-future.mdc` for the indexing script vision using TypeScript.

